WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.425
Every Google Cloud CLI command runs with
an identity.

2
00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:10.649
That identity may be a signed-in user,
service account,

3
00:00:10.729 --> 00:00:17.624
metadata-based identity, workload
identity, federated CI/CD identity,

4
00:00:17.704 --> 00:00:20.877
or impersonated service account.

5
00:00:20.957 --> 00:00:27.852
The identity determines what the script
can read, create, update, deploy,

6
00:00:27.932 --> 00:00:29.355
or administer.

7
00:00:29.435 --> 00:00:35.126
IAM design is therefore central to Google
Cloud CLI security.

8
00:00:35.206 --> 00:00:38.380
Permissions should match the job.

9
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.713
A reporting script should not use a
deployment identity.

10
00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:51.016
A validation step should not need the same
permissions as a production change.

11
00:00:51.096 --> 00:00:58.976
A rollback workflow should not
automatically share broad owner-level permissions.

12
00:00:59.056 --> 00:01:06.170
Least privilege should consider
organization, folder, project, resource,

13
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:12.379
role, action, condition where available,
environment, and time.

14
00:01:12.459 --> 00:01:20.339
Service account impersonation can reduce
service account key-file exposure when it

15
00:01:20.419 --> 00:01:22.607
is governed carefully.

16
00:01:22.687 --> 00:01:28.926
It should be intentional, auditable, and
scoped to a clear purpose.

17
00:01:29.006 --> 00:01:35.572
Teams should understand who or what can
impersonate a service account,

18
00:01:35.652 --> 00:01:43.094
which scripts use it, what that service
account can do, and how usage is reviewed.

19
00:01:43.174 --> 00:01:48.427
Impersonation is not a shortcut around
least privilege;

20
00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:55.074
it is a way to manage runtime access
without spreading long-lived keys.

21
00:01:55.154 --> 00:02:00.407
Avoid broad shared Owner or Editor access
in automation.

22
00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:04.646
Broad roles may make early scripting
easier,

23
00:02:04.726 --> 00:02:09.870
but they make mistakes and incidents
harder to contain.

24
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:16.407
Each automation identity should have an
owner, a documented purpose,

25
00:02:16.487 --> 00:02:20.646
a known usage location, and a review
process.

26
00:02:20.726 --> 00:02:24.337
Stale roles, unused service accounts,

27
00:02:24.417 --> 00:02:29.780
and overbroad permissions should be
cleaned up over time.
